Saint Lucia Population and Housing Census - 2022
Foreword
The 2022 Population and Housing Census marks a significant milestone in the Central Statistical
Office's (CSO) ongoing statistical activities to unravel our small island developing state demographic,
social, and economic dynamics
Conducting a population and housing census (PHC) is a comprehensive, complex, and costly statistical
undertaking to provide a snapshot of a country’s population distribution at a specific point in time.
Census data serves as a benchmark for a country’s demographic statistics, from the national level down
to the smallest statistical geographical unit, the "enumeration district" (ED).
The data collection for the 2022 census saw a transition from the Paper-Assisted Personal Interview
(PAPI) to the Computer-Assisted Personal Interview (CAPI) approach, improving the capturing,
management, and organization of large datasets, and ensuring data quality and accuracy. Preparations
began in 2019, guided by recommendations from the UN ECLAC User Guide for Population and
Housing Censuses - Rev 3. The COVID-19 pandemic interrupted critical components of the
preparatory phase, leading to a twelve-month hiatus of all census activities. Despite these challenges,
with additional resources from the Government of Saint Lucia, the Central Statistical Office was able
to meet the revised timeline for completing the census activities.
Stakeholders and international partners can be assured that the 2022 census was designed using best
practices, tailored to reflect the unique characteristics and needs of the general public. The pre-
enumeration process included comprehensive training for census personnel, by adopting innovative
data collection strategies combined with active community engagement throughout the EDs to ensure
thorough coverage of households.
This report will be made available in a series of three releases. The results in this report detail
population size and structure by enumeration district, annual population growth rates, population
density, health, education, and population standard of living. The census tables in this document are
CARICOM Regional Census Strategy - 2020 Round of Population compliant and conform to the
recommendations for publishing census data for the Caribbean region.
The Statistics Department is delighted to provide the census information that we believe is crucial for
effective data-driven governance and equitable resource distribution and to support informed decision-
making in policy, economic planning, and social services.
The census data was analyzed by applying geographical and spatial information to examine variations
in demographic and socio-economic factors, utilizing multiple levels of statistical analysis to derive
meaningful insights into statistical trends and patterns within the Saint Lucian space. Hyperlinks are
provided for easy navigation of this report, comments are welcome.
Sean C. Mathurin
Director of Statistics
