Saint Lucia Population and Housing Census - 2022
Introduction
The insights presented in this report stem from a rigorous analysis of data collected during Saint
Lucia’s 2022 Population and Housing Census. These results were carefully derived utilizing a process
of data cleaning and rigorous consistency verification. This work is under review, and any changes
will be communicated to users in a subsequent release. The Central Statistical Office endeavors to add
relevant tables and other forms of information dissemination as the census dataset undergoes further
analysis, in keeping with prescribed regional and international standards for releasing a more extensive
set of results.
Additionally, definitional, and other analytical variations may affect the comparisons between the
2022 and previous censuses. Therefore, users of census information should approach the related tables
with discernment.
The differences in definition between terms such as “Household Population” and “Total Population”
must also be considered. Note that the Household Population is the sum of all people recorded on
visitation records and/or electronic census questionnaires, on which a set of geographically dependent
weight factors has been applied to arrive at estimated full values. The Total Population is the household
population plus an estimate of Long-time residents who on census night, 12/13 March 2022, resided
in institutions for collective living such as hospitals, hotels, and correctional institutions.
The household enumeration encountered challenges of undercounting due to reasons like non-
cooperation from respondents. To estimate undercounts, some countries conduct post-enumeration
surveys, a cost-prohibitive process that proved resource-intensive for a small nation-state like Saint
Lucia. For the 2022 Saint Lucia census, the Department's Mapping and Listing operations achieved
better coverage of households than the actual household count. These operations uncovered significant
discrepancies in the reported numbers of inhabited buildings, dwellings, and households. This
undercount varied across enumeration districts, leading to the application of weighted factors to adjust
population and housing statistics.
The provisional results from the 2022 census indicate an increase in Saint Lucia's resident population,
reaching a total of 172,948 individuals, compared to the count of 2010. The population density now
stands at approximately 721 persons per square mile. The increase in population contrasts sharply with
the mid-year population estimates of 182,289 for 2022 (Mid-Year Estimates). The aggregate is derived
by factoring in births, deaths, and migration data to the previous year’s estimate1. The age dependency
ratio declined from 46.47 in 2010 to 44.5 in 2022. The sex ratio decreased to 97.2 from 101.5. Health
concerns include a high prevalence of diabetes and hypertension. Geographic distribution trends reveal
Castries remains the most populated district despite a decline observed for the census count, while
Gros Islet, Vieux Fort, Laborie, and Choiseul, saw significant growth. Household size has decreased
over time, and educational attainment shows a sizable portion of the population lacks formal education.
Crime statistics indicate theft as the most common offense, and the population pyramids illustrate a
youthful demographic.
Census 2022 can be described as a “De jure” census. People were enumerated at their usual place of
residence during census night, which means that absent household members were included in the
enumeration exercise.
1 Due to an inefficient National Statistical System (NSS), the required births, deaths and migration data is not easily
obtainable and provided timely. The NSS consist of the CSO and all other producers of official statistics in St. Lucia.
