groups 30 -34 and 35-39 reported a higher percentage of females.
Saint Lucia Population Density
Provisional Census results for 2022 indicate that Saint Lucia’s population density is 721 people per
square mile (Table A.6). This figure was derived using the land mass area of 238.2 square miles, which
includes the country’s forest reserve areas. This forest reserve areas cover approximately 30 square
miles. The enumeration district of Castries had a population density of 1,529 people per square mile
(Table A.6). Canaries the least densely populated district, has a density of 231 people per square mile.
The district of Gros Islet experienced a rapid population expansion, recording a population density of
773 individuals per square mile, up from 643 in 2010. In 1991, census data reported a population
density of 345 persons per square mile for the enumeration district of Gros Islet.
Age Dependency Ratio
The Age Dependency Ratio is a measure used to assess the pressure on the “productive population”
by comparing the number of “dependents” (young and old) to the working-age population. That
number is typically expressed as a percentage. The age dependency ratio is calculated using the
following approach,
ADR = (Number of Dependents /Working Age Population) ×100 Where: Number of Dependents
includes individuals classified as young dependents (typically under age 15) and elderly dependents
age 65 and over. The working-age population consists of individuals aged 15 to 64.
The age dependency ratio based on 2022 census data is 44.5 (Figure 1). This figure places Saint Lucia
among countries with a lower age dependency ratio, indicating that a smaller proportion of the
population depends on the working-age population. The enumeration districts with the highest
prevalence of dependent population are Micoud, Soufriere, and Choiseul (Table A.3). The ADR places
Saint Lucia in position 35 in the World Bank’s ranking of 240 ADR countries.
