Sex Ratio
The overall sex ratio for 2022 was 97.2 (Table A.3). Notably, the sex ratio was higher in rural
communities of Canaries and Anse La Raye for 2022. Compared to 2010, the sex ratio was 98.5,
indicating a slight decrease in males per 100 females. A decline in the sex ratio signals a demographic
shift in population trends brought about by higher female birth rates, increased male mortality, and
increased emigration flows. Changes in the sex ratio have implications for Saint Lucia’s social
dynamics, health, and social services for balanced and equitable development.
Population Health
Diabetes and Hypertension continue to rank as the highest NCDs on the island with the prevalence of
diabetes in the enumeration districts of Castries and Gros Islet and Hypertension; Castries, Gros Islet,
Micoud, and Vieux Fort4 (Table D.4). This raises concerns about the widespread occurrence of these
NCDs and their impact on the population, productivity, mental factors such as stress and depression,
resource constraints, diet, and lifestyle. The high prevalence of medical conditions such as heart
disease, cardiovascular disease, asthma, and depression in the Castries enumeration district (Table
D.4), places pressure on the healthcare infrastructure particularly, for access to specialized care and
improved socioeconomic conditions and removal of barriers to healthcare.
Geographic Distribution and Population Movement
Geographic Distribution of Population
Provisional data from St. Lucia 2022 Population and Housing Census show that the largest proportion
of Saint Lucia’s population resides in Castries (Table A.2). Even with a contraction of 7.7% (Table
A.5), Castries still represents a substantial proportion (35.3%) of Saint Lucia’s total population, while
the lowest populations concentration is observed in Anse-La-Raye and Canaries. Districts like Micoud
and Dennery have stable populations with notable slight increases. The population distribution has
experienced a subtle yet noteworthy shift, with areas like Gros Islet and Vieux Fort becoming more
prominent. Consequently, these areas could see significant development and growth, reshaping the
social and economic landscape of Saint Lucia
Population Movement
Successive population counts show a substantial migration of people from Castries to Gros Islet
district, Gros Islet has the second most populated district with a population share of 17.4% for 2022,
up from 15.2% in 2010 (Table A.2). This represented an 18.8% increase in population growth, rising
from 25,210 in 2010 to 29,953 in 2022. The least populated district in Saint Lucia is the enumeration
district of Canaries, accounting for 1.3% of the total population of Saint Lucia. Among the ten districts,
seven districts experienced population growth. Anse La Raye, Canaries, and Soufriere recorded
declining growth rates (Table A.2) for the intercensal period. The population movement from 2010 to
2022 demonstrates some interesting trends. The total household population grew by 3.8%, from
165,595 in 2010 to 171,834 in 2022.
According to Table A.2, while most districts saw an increased number of households, many also
experienced decreases in household size. Some districts, like Castries and Anse La Raye, faced a
population decline despite a notable increase in household total, suggesting migration or demographic
4 These numbers represent reported cases, there may be additional undiagnosed or unreported cases.
