changes such as an aging population. In contrast, districts like Choiseul, Laborie, Vieux Fort, and Gros
Islet saw substantial population and household growth, indicating these areas might be more attractive
for settlement or have better living conditions and opportunities.
Previous censuses showed considerable internal migration from the southern districts to the north, a
trend that has slowed down over the intercensal period. In terms of international migration during the
intercensal years, among those who resided abroad and since returned to Saint Lucia, 3,000 of the
emigrants were born abroad. The total number of recorded emigrants stands at 3,236 (Table C.2). The
disparity between the two totals can be explained as an underestimation of emigrant totals since the
departure of households would not be noticeable in the census, given those households were not present
to be enumerated. The net migration rate is comparatively small relative to Saint Lucia's population
size, as there is a small excess of departures recorded.
The movement of individuals is primarily influenced by factors such as family migration, economic
opportunities, and the various free movement arrangements within the CARICOM framework (Annex
Table 7.1). Most of the immigrant population comprises individuals migrating for family-related
reasons—such as those accompanying parents during childhood, and those relocating for work-related
reasons. Economic migration, in particular, exhibits a higher prevalence among males. Alternatively,
migration motivated by unspecified reasons and humanitarian circumstances shows a higher
prevalence among females. Immigration cases linked to natural disasters, humanitarian situations, and
deportation are rare.
Household Size and Structure
In total, 65,951 households were recorded in the 2022 Population and Housing Census, compared to
58,891 households in May 2010. This represents an increase of 7,060 residential units, or 12%, over
the intercensal period (Table A.5). Despite a modest Total Resident population increase of 3.9% (Table
A.1), the average household size has decreased significantly, dropping from 2.8 per household in 2010
to 2.6 in 2022 ( Table A.5).
The average household size has steadily declined during the intercensal years, from 4.0 people per
household in 1991 to 3.3 in 2001, 2.8 in 2010, and 2.6 in 2022. At the district level, the average
household size varied, with Soufriere at 2.8 and Canaries at 2.5. A comparison of the 2022 and 2010
censuses reveals a decline in average household size across all enumeration districts in Saint Lucia -
Laborie experiencing the most significant decrease, from 3.1 people per household in 2010 to 2.6 in
2022 (Table A.5).
Education
Based on (Table D.5), we observe that the educational landscape exhibits several notable features and
weaknesses. It is important to note that most of the population achieved only primary or secondary
education as of Census Day, 12/13 March 2022 (Table 4). The data shows that 10,446 individuals have
no formal schooling - a gap in early childhood education access or retention. A small fraction of the
population holds a university degree, and educational attainment varies throughout the ten enumeration
districts. Gros Islet has a larger percentage of individuals with tertiary education (Table D.5). The gap
in male-to-female university education can be attributed to several factors in males acquiring degrees.
