Economically Active Population
Annex (Table 9.10) presents employment status data segmented by age group and sex. The categories
are "Worked for pay, profit or family gain," "Worked for tending household, children, etc. "Did not
work," and "Not reported."
From Figure 12 (1) we observe that a larger proportion of the Economically Active Population is
concentrated in the younger to middle-aged groups, specifically 20-49 years. There is a sharp decline
in population as age increases, particularly beyond 50 years, which is typical as mortality rates increase
with aging. The graph highlights the aging pattern in the population, showing fewer people in the older
age groups.
Employment status is heavily influenced by age, with the highest employment rates in the 30-49 age
range (Figure 12 (2). Females are more likely to be involved in household duties compared to males.
From Figure 12 (2) the employment rate drops sharply in the older age groups (60+).
